---
title: Shopify Mobile Product Page Checklist - 12 Fixes
description: >-
  A 12-point checklist for your Shopify mobile product page, from add to cart placement to delivery dates, that you can work through today.
date: 2026-11-27
category: shopify
draft: false
---
Most visits to a Shopify store happen on a phone, and phones convert worse than desktops. One benchmark puts the [average Shopify mobile conversion rate at 1.2% against 1.9% on desktop](https://redstagfulfillment.com/average-conversion-rate-for-shopify-stores/). Part of that gap is behaviour: people browse on mobile and buy later. A lot of it is the page.

Open one of your best-selling product pages on your own phone and go through this list. Each item is something I check in every product page audit.

## Above the fold

### 1. Price and add to cart reachable without scrolling far

If a shopper has to scroll past four images and a long description to find the add to cart button, many never get there. Either keep the buy box close to the top or add a sticky add to cart bar that appears once the main button scrolls out of view.

### 2. The product name and one clear reason to buy

Under the title, one short line that says what makes this product different. Not a paragraph, one line.

### 3. Image gallery that swipes, not taps

Swiping between images should work, and the first image should show the product clearly, not a lifestyle shot where the product is small.

## Choosing options

### 4. Variant buttons, not dropdowns

Size and colour as visible buttons are faster to use than a dropdown that opens a tiny native picker. Show which options are sold out instead of letting someone pick one and fail.

### 5. A size guide right next to the size options

If you sell anything with sizes, the size guide link belongs beside the size buttons, and it should open on the same page.

## Removing doubt

### 6. Delivery date, not just "shipping"

"Arrives by Thursday" converts better than "ships in 3 to 5 days". Show the estimate near the add to cart button.

### 7. Shipping cost and return policy visible before the cart

Surprise costs at checkout are a top reason carts get abandoned. Say "free shipping over $50" and "30-day returns" on the product page, close to the price.

### 8. Reviews summary near the top

The star rating and number of reviews just under the title, linking down to the full reviews. Real reviews only; shoppers notice when they all sound the same.

### 9. Offer visible on the product page

If there is a sale or a discount code, show it on the product page itself. Many stores put the offer on the homepage banner and lose it everywhere else.

## Speed and basics

### 10. Fast on a normal phone connection

Test the page with Google PageSpeed Insights on mobile. Large uncompressed images and too many apps loading scripts are the usual causes of a slow product page.

### 11. Tap targets big enough

Buttons and links should be easy to hit with a thumb. If you often tap the wrong option, so do your customers.

### 12. Tracking that actually works

Before and after changes, check that view_item, add_to_cart and purchase events arrive in GA4 once each. Otherwise you cannot tell what any of these fixes did. [Duplicate purchases are a common problem](/blog/shopify-ga4-purchase-event-firing-twice/).

## Where to start

Do the checks on your top three products by traffic. Fix anything that is clearly broken straight away. For changes where you are not sure which version is better, such as a new gallery layout, consider testing if you [have the traffic for it](/blog/shopify-ab-testing-traffic-requirements/).

If you want these done for you, a [Shopify store audit](/services/shopify-audits/) goes through your product page, cart and mobile journey and ships the fixes, not just a report. You can also start with a [free 3-leak video audit](/#mini-audit).
